{"cuisine/turkmen-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/turkmen-cuisine","n":"Turkmen cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Turkmenistan","intro":[["Turkmen cuisine, the cuisine of Turkmenistan, is similar to that of the rest of Central Asia. Turkmen seminomadic culture revolved around animal husbandry, especially sheep herding, and accordingly Turkmen cuisine is noted for its focus on meat, particularly mutton and lamb. One source notes, The nomadic past has left a very noticeable trace in Turkmen cuisine - the basis of the diet is meat: lamb, meat of gazelles, non-working camels, wild fowl, chicken. Beef is consumed much less frequently because this food appeared on the table much later, Turkmens don't eat horse meat at all."],["Turkmen cuisine does not generally use spices or seasonings other than salt and black pepper, and is typically cooked with large amounts of widely available cottonseed oil."],["A description of Turkmen foods presented at an annual culinary festival included \".. more than 15 kinds of soup, meat and fish delicacies, ruddy ichlekli (meat pies), appetizing gutaps with different fillings (pumpkin, spinach), crumbly pilaf, kelle bash ayak and chekdirme, whole roasted lamb, kakmach, hearty yarma, numerous salads, traditional pishme, as well as sweets..\" At a formal ribbon-cutting ceremony for new housing, the offerings included \"..all kinds of meat, poultry and fish dishes, cereals, vegetables and gourds, soups, culinary products made of dough, dairy products and drinks prepared according to ancient recipes. These include tamdyrlama, ichlekli, yarma, dograma, different kinds of pilaf, çorba, somsa, pishme, süzme, çal, agaran and much else.\""]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"turkmen-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turkmen_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":4,"links":[["Beshbarmak","dish/beshbarmak"],["Chorba","dish/chorba"],["Kuurdak","dish/kuurdak"],["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Red Sands: Reportage and Recipes Through Central Asia, from Hinterland to Heartland","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Caroline Eden, 2020","note":"Travel writing and recipes across the five Central Asian republics, not this cuisine alone.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Noodles","ingredient/noodles"],["Beans","ingredient/beans"],["Legumes","ingredient/legumes"],["Vegetables","ingredient/vegetables"],["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Animal fats and oils","ingredient/animal-fats-and-oils"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Dried fruit","ingredient/dried-fruit"],["Lard","ingredient/lard"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Stock (cooking)","ingredient/stock-cooking"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/ac/Turkmen-meal-on-sachak.jpg/500px-Turkmen-meal-on-sachak.jpg","w":500,"h":398,"by":"Amustard","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkmen-meal-on-sachak.jpg"}},"cuisine/romani-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/romani-cuisine","n":"Romani cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Romania","intro":[["Romani cuisine is diverse, reflecting the culinary traditions of the respective countries in which Romani people have lived for generations. However, distinctive recipes have emerged within Romani populations over time. In Southeastern Europe, Romani cuisine is part of the broader Balkan culinary tradition, with profound Ottoman Turkish influence. Stuffed cabbage and stuffed peppers are widely prepared. In Romania, cornmeal is a frequent dish. Ian Hancock dubbed Romani cuisine the “soul food of Europe”."],["Some Roma hold that certain foods are auspicious, or lucky (baxtalo), especially foods with pungent tastes such as garlic, lemon, tomato, and peppers, and fermented foods such as sauerkraut, pickles and sour cream. Hedgehogs were historically a delicacy among some Romanis. Traditionally, many Romani would avoid eating food prepared by non-Romani. Tea is widely enjoyed, prepared with peaches and strawberries. Frybread is another favored dish. Romani dishes may retain influences from their historically nomadic lifestyle."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"romani-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romani_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Gypsy Feast: Recipes and Culinary Traditions of the Romany People","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Carol Wilson","note":"Hippocrene collection of Romani recipes and food customs from across Europe.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780781810272","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780781810272"}]},{"h":"More from Romania","count":2,"links":[["Romanian cuisine","cuisine/romanian-cuisine"],["Transylvanian Saxon cuisine","cuisine/transylvanian-saxon-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/3e/Olla_gitana.jpg/500px-Olla_gitana.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Retama","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olla_gitana.jpg"}},"dish/battenberg-cake":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/battenberg-cake","n":"Battenberg cake","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from British cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Battenberg or Battenburg cake is a light sponge cake with coloured sections held together with jam and covered in marzipan. In cross section, the cake has a distinctive pink-and-yellow check pattern. It originated in England."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-battenberg-cake"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battenberg_cake"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Jam","ingredient/jam"],["Marzipan","ingredient/marzipan"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Great British Chefs: traditional British recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"greatbritishchefs.com","note":"UK chef-driven recipe site with a large section of classic British dishes.","url":"https://www.greatbritishchefs.com/features/our-most-popular-traditional-british-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from British cuisine","links":[["Banbury cake","dish/banbury-cake"],["Black pudding","dish/black-pudding"],["Bread sauce","dish/bread-sauce"],["Carpetbag steak","dish/carpetbag-steak"],["Chelsea bun","dish/chelsea-bun"],["Christmas pudding","dish/christmas-pudding"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Crumble","dish/crumble"],["Crumpet","dish/crumpet"],["Curry in the United Kingdom","dish/curry-in-the-united-kingdom"],["Digestive biscuit","dish/digestive-biscuit"],["Dundee cake","dish/dundee-cake"],["English muffin","dish/english-muffin"],["Flies' graveyard","dish/flies-graveyard"],["Flummery","dish/flummery"],["Fritter","dish/fritter"],["Garibaldi biscuit","dish/garibaldi-biscuit"],["Hot cross bun","dish/hot-cross-bun"],["Hovis biscuit","dish/hovis-biscuit"],["Jammie Dodgers","dish/jammie-dodgers"],["Melba toast","dish/melba-toast"],["Mince pie","dish/mince-pie"],["Phall","dish/phall"],["Pickled egg","dish/pickled-egg"],["Pie","dish/pie"],["Ploughman's lunch","dish/ploughman-s-lunch"],["Potted shrimps","dish/potted-shrimps"],["Prawn cocktail, steak and Black Forest gateau","dish/prawn-cocktail-steak-and-black-forest-gateau"],["Rich tea","dish/rich-tea"],["Sausage roll","dish/sausage-roll"],["Scotch egg","dish/scotch-egg"],["Shepherd's pie","dish/shepherd-s-pie"],["Soldiers (food)","dish/soldiers-food"],["Spotted dick","dish/spotted-dick"],["Tea sandwich","dish/tea-sandwich"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/ed/Battenbergcake.jpg/500px-Battenbergcake.jpg","w":500,"h":252,"by":"Henrycooksey","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battenbergcake.jpg"}},"dish/chhanamukhi":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/chhanamukhi","n":"Chhanamukhi","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Bangladeshi cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Chhanamukhi is a Bangladeshi sweet. It originated in the Brahmanbaria District. It is made with fried cottage cheese and sugar syrup. On September 24, 2024, Chanamukhi Sweet got recognition as a Geographical Indication (GI) product."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-chhanamukhi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chhanamukhi"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Bangladeshi cuisine","cuisine/bangladeshi-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Chhena","ingredient/chhena"],["Maida (flour)","ingredient/maida-flour"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]},{"h":"More from Bangladeshi cuisine","links":[["Durus kura","dish/durus-kura"],["Shemai","dish/shemai"],["Shorshe ilish","dish/shorshe-ilish"],["Thali","dish/thali"],["Zarda (food)","dish/zarda-food"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/72/Chanamukhi01.jpg/500px-Chanamukhi01.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Bellayet","lic":"CC BY 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chanamukhi01.jpg"}},"ingredient/cumin-seed":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/cumin-seed","n":"Cumin seed","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Cumin is a flowering plant in the family Apiaceae, native to the Irano-Turanian Region. Its seeds – each one contained within a fruit, which is dried – are used in the cuisines of many cultures in both whole and ground form. Although cumin is used in traditional medicine, there is no high-quality evidence that it is safe or effective as a therapeutic agent."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-cumin-seed"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cumin_seed"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Parsi cuisine","cuisine/parsi-cuisine",[["Dhansak","dish/dhansak"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Gourd","ingredient/gourd"],["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Lentil","ingredient/lentil"],["Pumpkin","ingredient/pumpkin"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/5/58/Cuminum_cyminum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-198.jpg","w":500,"h":660,"by":"Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuminum_cyminum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-198.jpg"}},"ingredient/beta":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/beta","n":"Beta","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Beta is a genus of flowering plants in the family Amaranthaceae.  The best known member is the common beet, Beta vulgaris, but several other species are recognised.  Almost all have common names containing the word \"beet\". Wild Beta species can be found throughout the Atlantic coast of Europe, the Mediterranean coastline, the Near East, and parts of Asia including India."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-beta"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beta_(plant)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine",[["Pkhali","dish/pkhali"]]],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Dressed herring","dish/dressed-herring"]]],["Soviet cuisine","cuisine/soviet-cuisine",